A||N||12||reddog||best set up for 17" wheels ||||5||
Z||000000||reddog||10-02-2001||08:50 PM||||I have decided to upgrade to 17" wheels and was wondering what the best set up would be. Have been considering Tenzo mean 6 can any one tell me what off set I will need for the front and the back as well as what tire size (I.E. 205/40/17,225/45/17???ETC.)I haven't lowered the car YET!!! will be doing that in the near future.<P><BR>86 GT 4-speed Auto (will be 5-speed next month)||||reg||5
Z||000001||reddog||10-02-2001||10:27 PM||||CAN ANYONE HELP? PLEASE||||reg||9
Z||000002||mrfiero||10-02-2001||10:42 PM||||Look for offsets in the 40mm range (the more the merrier)......that will help "tuck" the wheel inside the fender.<P>As for what sizes to run......a lot of people I know with 17's are using 215/45's up front and 235/45's in the rear.  <P>You could possibly go wider or a lower aspect ratio, but these sizes are more in line with the "plus" upgrades (that is they are closer to the original height of the stock wheels/tires).  <P>I hope this helps.    ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||10-02-2001||10:48 PM||||Since no one is helping.......<P>I have TR motorsports Typhoons , I have also had 3 other sets (just trying them) of 17" wheels on my car.<P>Typically the vast majority of the "Tuner" Rim market is comprised of 43mm offset rims. This is perfect for a fiero.<P>As for tires..<P>Rear , on a 7" rim ..235/45/17<P>215/40's front.<P>With the 84 to 87 suspension , you want a good spread in width between front and rear tire (a lot like a 911) this combo will also give the car a bit more rake , thus moving the center of gravity foreward ..ie less understeer.<BR>Hope this helps.<P>JM<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||reddog||10-02-2001||10:50 PM||||Thanks for the help I was begining to think no one was going to respond! Do you know of anyone that has any really good deals going on right now?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rick 88||10-02-2001||11:43 PM||||On my 88 I am running a 215/45/17 front and a 245/45/17 rear with no problems. My front is lowered one inch. The wheels are 17x7.5's.<BR>To get some good package prices check out <A HREF="http://www.Victoriatire.com" TARGET=_blank>www.Victoriatire.com</A>  . I purchase my set mounted/balanced, and delivered to my door by UPS. I got a better price and saved the sales tax. <P>Rick 88||||reg||3
Z||000006||artherd||10-03-2001||02:54 AM||||215/45/17 on 7" front wheels.<P>245/40/17 on 8" rear wheels.<BR>(optionally 135 or 225/45/17 on 7" rears)<P>~40mm offset, 5x100mm bolt pattern.<P>This is how the fiero would come *today* from the factory, and it's a dream to drive with the proper rooling-stock!!<P>Do it, you will LOVE the change, like getting a whole new car.<P>Oddly enough, my ride didn't even get harsher, on the contrary, it just smoothed out.  I absolutely love it, I'm going to be trying a set of 18s next.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000007||onfire||10-03-2001||03:36 AM||||I have 215/40's all around. I plan on going to 235 in the rears, when it needs new tires. Maybe wider, not sure yet. Other things on my mind. Like suspension, power, big brakes, power, interior, oh yeah did I say power. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||onfire||10-04-2001||01:07 PM||||Here is a picture of my wheels. With no suspension modifications. It needs to be lowered. What do you guys think?<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bigwheels17.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/onfiresigtoo.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by onfire (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rick 88||10-04-2001||02:52 PM||||The wheels look nice! Definitely go bigger tires in the back when its time for new ones.<P>Rick 88||||reg||14
Z||000010||Alex4mula||10-04-2001||03:03 PM||||215-45-17 front 235-40-17 rear both 7" rim. These sizes keep tire diameter almost the same as stock. I think if you use &gt;225-45-17 in the rear the tire sidewall is/look too big. But people like that sometimes.||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||10-04-2001||04:14 PM||||get 18s..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>if the people you are ordering from flake and choke when you say fiero.. say uum i ment Cavilier.  40-42mm  to 7.5" wide wheel, 45mm for 8-9"  17s. on 18s.. 45mm 8". <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstmeat3.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstmeat3.jpg</A> <P>for wheel/tires check out <A HREF="http://www.wheel1.com" TARGET=_blank>www.wheel1.com</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||reddog||10-04-2001||08:02 PM|||| Thanks for everyone's input, I have decided to go with Street Rebels 17's with 235/45/17 on the rear and 215/45/17 on the front. Ordered them today they should be in at the first of the week. will post pictures when I get them on.||||reg||12
